VERIFY-FIRST — terra finding 2 (U+FEFF: POSIX whitespace class vs ECMAScript trim)
AI4DEV-65 (who signed fields), FIX AND GOAL sitting, executor.
Ruling that ordered these probes: loop/items/AI4DEV-65/gate2-rulings.md, "Terra [2] P3 — verify
first, both branches pre-ruled".

OUTCOME: BRANCH B HELD. Probe 1 returned false. U+FEFF is NOT in PostgreSQL's POSIX whitespace
class on the slot-1 deployment, and ECMAScript trim() DOES strip it in the suite's runtime. The
divergence terra names is real on this deployment. Per the ruling, no constraint changes; the
boundary statement ordered under terra finding 1 gains the one ruled sentence.

================================================================================
WHICH DATABASE
================================================================================

Slot 1 of the local database slot pool — the same stack the integration tier occupies with
AT_DB_SLOT=1. The slot's database port is derived by the pool's own overlay rule: the item tree's
supabase/config.toml carries [db] port = 54322, and tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts maps a listener
port in 54000-54999 by +1000 per slot, so slot 1 listens on 55322. Confirmed independently by
docker:

  PS> docker port supabase_db_ai4good-slot-1
  5432/tcp -> 0.0.0.0:55322
  5432/tcp -> [::]:55322

55322 is outside the founder's personal port block (54320-54329). Both probes are READ-ONLY. The
probe script ran from the scratchpad, outside the item tree, and was deleted afterwards.

================================================================================
PROBE 1 — the database. Is U+FEFF in PostgreSQL's POSIX whitespace class?
================================================================================

Statement sent, verbatim:

  select U&'\FEFF' ~ '^\s*$' as feff_is_posix_blank

Driver: bun's SQL client (Bun.SQL), connection
postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:55322/postgres.

Command, verbatim:

  PS> bun "C:\Users\nirdr\AppData\Local\Temp\claude\C--Users-nirdr-Downloads-ai4good\007dee8b-00c5-4a4a-ac9c-cc583206d805\scratchpad\feff-probe.ts"

The script, verbatim (deleted after the run; it is reproduced here so the reading can be repeated):

  // Throwaway probe, outside the item tree. Read-only, slot 1 only (port 55322).
  const port = 55322;
  if (port >= 54320 && port <= 54329) throw new Error('refusing: personal port block');
  const SQL = (globalThis as { Bun?: { SQL?: new (url: string) => any } }).Bun?.SQL;
  if (!SQL) throw new Error('no bun SQL client');
  const sql = new SQL(`postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:${port}/postgres`);
  try {
    const rows = await sql`select U&'\FEFF' ~ '^\s*$' as feff_is_posix_blank`;
    console.log(JSON.stringify(rows));
  } finally {
    await sql.close().catch(() => undefined);
  }

Output, verbatim:

  [{"feff_is_posix_blank":false}]

Reading: FALSE. A string of nothing but U+FEFF does NOT match '^\s*$', so the columns' check
constraints — signer_name !~ '^\s*$' and its two siblings — accept it. The database's blank floor
does not treat U+FEFF as blank.

================================================================================
PROBE 2 — the JS runtime. Does trim() strip U+FEFF?
================================================================================

Command, verbatim:

  PS> bun -e "console.log(JSON.stringify({ trimmedIsEmpty: '\uFEFF'.trim() === '', codePoint: '\uFEFF'.codePointAt(0), trimmedLength: '\uFEFF'.trim().length, runtime: 'bun ' + Bun.version }))"

The character is written as the JavaScript escape \uFEFF rather than as the raw character. Windows
PowerShell does not expand \u in a double-quoted string, so bun received the escape and JavaScript
resolved it. The reported codePoint 65279 is 0xFEFF, which proves the string under test is the one
the ruling names.

Output, verbatim:

  {"trimmedIsEmpty":true,"codePoint":65279,"trimmedLength":0,"runtime":"bun 1.3.14"}

Reading: TRUE. A U+FEFF-only string trims to the empty string in the suite's runtime, and the
trimmed length is 0. So validateCompleteSignup's presence-and-blank checks refuse a FEFF-only
signer field: the value trims to the empty string and the check names its field.

================================================================================
WHAT THE TWO READINGS TOGETHER MEAN
================================================================================

The two blank floors disagree, and they disagree in the safe direction for every caller that goes
through the edge function. Through the deployed path a FEFF-only value never reaches the database:
the validation layer trims it to empty and refuses the completion. The only writer that can persist
a FEFF-only signer field is a caller holding the service key that calls public.complete_signup
directly and bypasses the edge function — the same trusted-key caller, and the same boundary, that
terra finding 1 describes.

Per the ruling this is an accepted residual, stated rather than moved. Chasing the ECMAScript
whitespace set through PostgreSQL character classes would reproduce, character by character, the
two-source drift hazard that finding 1 rejects.

